CH = dict(num=5, title="New Beginnings: Cities and States", tag="History", theme="Second Urbanisation, mahajanapadas, early republics, varna-jati",
 intro="After the Harappan cities faded, India had no urban life for about a thousand years. From the 1st millennium BCE, cities rose again in the Ganga plains, along with organised states called janapadas and mahajanapadas. This 'Second Urbanisation' brought iron tools, coins, trade routes, new ideas and one of the world's earliest republics.",
 big_questions=["What is meant by the 'Second Urbanisation of India'?", "Why were janapadas and mahajanapadas important in early Indian history?", "What system of governance did they evolve?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "5.1 From First to Second Urbanisation"),
 ("ul", ["The Indus / Harappan / Sindhu-Sarasvati civilisation (**First Urbanisation**) declined in the early 2nd millennium BCE: planned cities, markets, specialised crafts, writing, sanitation, administration and the ruling class vanished; people reverted to village life.",
  "For nearly a **thousand years** urban life was largely absent (a few towns only).",
  "In the **1st millennium BCE** a new urban phase began in the **Ganga plains**, parts of the Indus basin and nearby regions, then spread. It is the **Second Urbanisation**, which, in a way, continues today.",
  "**Sources**: (1) **archaeological excavations** of ancient urban centres; (2) **literature** — late Vedic, Buddhist and Jain texts mention them."]),
 ("h2", "5.2 Janapadas and Mahajanapadas"),
 ("ul", ["Towards the end of the 2nd millennium BCE clans (sharing language and customs) became linked to territories called **janapadas**, ruled by a **raja**. *Janapada* = 'where the people (*jana*) have set foot (*pada*)', i.e. settled.",
  "Trade networks helped them grow; by the **8th-7th centuries BCE** some merged into larger states: **mahajanapadas**. The usual list has **16**, from **Gandhara** (north-west) to **Anga** (east) and **Ashmaka** (central India near the Godavari).",
  "Many lay in the **Ganga plains** because of fertile land (farming), **iron ore** in nearby hills, and new trade networks.",
  "Most powerful: **Magadha, Kosala, Vatsa, Avanti**."]),
 ("table", ["Mahajanapada", "Capital (and note)", "Region"], [
  ["Magadha", "Rajagriha (modern Rajgir)", "Part of Bihar; most powerful"],
  ["Kosala", "Shravasti", "Part of Uttar Pradesh"],
  ["Vatsa", "Kaushambi (near Prayagraj)", "Uttar Pradesh"],
  ["Avanti", "Ujjayini (Ujjain)", "Part of Madhya Pradesh"],
  ["Gandhara", "Takshashila", "North-west"],
  ["Vrijji (Vajji)", "Vaishali", "Early republic (gana / sangha)"],
  ["Malla", "Kushinara", "Neighbour of Vrijji; early republic"],
  ["Anga", "Champa", "East"],
  ["Others", "Kuru (Indraprastha), Panchala (Ahichchhatra), Shurasena (Mathura), Matsya (Viratnagar), Chedi, Kamboja, Ashmaka", "—"]]),
 ("ul", ["Capitals were big, **fortified** cities with a **moat** (deep water-filled ditch) outside the ramparts and **narrow gateways** so guards could control the movement of people and goods. Many of those capitals are living cities 2,500 years later."]),
 ("h2", "5.3 Early Democratic Traditions"),
 ("ul", ["Each janapada had an assembly — **sabha** or **samiti** (the words appear in the Vedas), mostly of elders. The raja was expected to **take advice** from them and from ministers; texts even suggest an incompetent king could be removed (though this was not necessarily settled law, as evidence is incomplete)."]),
 ("table", ["Feature", "Monarchical mahajanapadas", "Ganas / Sanghas (Vrijji, Malla)"], [
  ["Authority", "Raja is the ultimate authority, supported by ministers and elders", "Sabha / samiti holds more power"],
  ["How decisions are taken", "By the king with advice", "Discussion and, if needed, **vote**"],
  ["Ruler", "**Hereditary** (usually son of previous king)", "**Selected** by the assembly"],
  ["Examples", "Magadha, Kosala, Avanti", "Vrijji and Malla: called **'early republics'**, among the earliest in the world"]]),
 ("ul", ["Duties of a king: collect taxes (revenue), keep law and order, build fortifications, maintain an army to defend or fight neighbours."]),
 ("h2", "5.4 Further Innovations"),
 ("ul", ["**New ideas**: late Vedic, Buddhist and Jain schools spread through scholars, monks, nuns and pilgrims; art was renewed (to blossom later under empires).",
  "**Iron metallurgy**: Harappans used copper and bronze. Iron extraction was perfected in several regions from the early 2nd millennium BCE and tools became widespread by the late 2nd millennium BCE. Iron tools allowed **farming on a bigger scale**, and lighter, sharper **weapons** (swords, spears, arrows, shields). Evidence of **warfare** between mahajanapadas; wars and alliances later produced kingdoms and empires.",
  "**Coins**: made necessary by growing trade. The first were of **silver**, punched with symbols — **punch-marked coins**; later copper, gold. Each mahajanapada issued its own, but coins circulated across regions and abroad."]),
 ("table", ["Feature", "First Urbanisation (Harappan)", "Second Urbanisation"], [
  ["Region", "Indus-Sarasvati valleys", "Ganga plains (spreading everywhere)"],
  ["Metal", "Copper / bronze", "**Iron** (also copper, bronze)"],
  ["Money", "Seals / barter-based trade", "**Punch-marked coins**"],
  ["Political structure", "Cities with administration", "Janapadas, mahajanapadas, ganas"],
  ["Writing / literature", "Script yet undeciphered", "Vedic, Buddhist, Jain literature"],
  ["Monasteries and warfare", "No", "Yes (monasteries; warfare between states)"]]),
 ("h2", "5.5 The Varna-Jati System"),
 ("ul", ["Complex societies develop groups by occupation or class. Ideally they complement each other, but they also create **inequality** (some groups gain more wealth and power).",
  "Indian society had a two-fold system: **Jati** = community defined by a specific occupation, skills passed down generations; may split into **sub-jatis** with their own customs (marriage, ritual, food). **Varna** = concept from Vedic texts with **four** varnas."]),
 ("table", ["Varna", "Role"], [
  ["Brahmins", "Preserve and spread knowledge; perform rituals"],
  ["Kshatriyas", "Defend society and land; fight when needed"],
  ["Vaishyas", "Increase wealth through trade, business, agriculture"],
  ["Shudras", "Artisans, craftspeople, workers, servants"]]),
 ("ul", ["The word **'caste'** comes from the Portuguese *casta* (16th-century Portuguese travellers). Scholars use it for varna, for jati, or for the whole system.",
  "In the early period people **changed occupations** when needed (e.g., drought sends farmers to cities; some Brahmins took to trade or military work). The system gave **stability** but later became **rigid**, causing inequality and discrimination against lower jatis and groups outside the system; it hardened especially under British rule. It is not the only mechanism of Indian society."]),
 ("h2", "5.6 Developments Elsewhere in India"),
 ("ul", ["Trade, pilgrimage and military routes opened up: **Uttarapatha** (north-west to Ganga plains and east) and **Dakshinapatha** (from **Kaushambi** across the **Vindhyas** to the south); lateral roads joined western and eastern ports.",
  "**Shishupalgarh** (Sisupalgarh, Bhubaneswar), capital of **Kalinga**: strict square plan, fortifications with narrow gateways, moat, broad streets (excavated from 1948).",
  "South India: cities appear from about **400 BCE**; kingdoms of the **Cholas, Cheras and Pandyas** (named in early Tamil literature). South was rich in gems, gold, spices and traded with India and overseas; **Kodumanal** (near Erode) had shell and gemstone industries.",
  "By **300-200 BCE** the whole Subcontinent, including the Northeast, was interlinked, with goods and culture reaching Central and Southeast Asia. About then the mahajanapadas ended, making way for empires."]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "Timeline: Harappan civilisation (about 1900 BCE) → janapadas (about 1200 BCE) → mahajanapadas (about 800 BCE) → Second Urbanisation in full swing → ends about 300 BCE. Kautilya's *Arthashastra* describes the ideal kingdom: fortified capital, fertile land, mines, forests, pastures, rivers not only rain, good roads and a productive economy."),
 ("h2", "5.7 Quick Revision"),
 ("qa", [("Why did urban life vanish after the Harappan civilisation?", "Its structures — administration, specialised crafts, markets, writing, sanitation and the ruling class — disappeared, and people returned to village life."),
  ("What is the Second Urbanisation and how do we know about it?", "A new phase of city growth in the 1st millennium BCE in the Ganga plains; known from excavations and from Vedic, Buddhist and Jain literature."),
  ("What does janapada mean?", "'Where the people have set foot', i.e. a territory settled by a clan."),
  ("How many mahajanapadas were there? Name the most powerful.", "Sixteen; Magadha, Kosala, Vatsa and Avanti were the most powerful."),
  ("Why were many mahajanapadas in the Ganga plains?", "Fertile land, iron ore in nearby hills and growing trade networks."),
  ("Describe the defence of a mahajanapada capital.", "A fortified city with rampart walls, a water-filled moat and narrow gateways to control entry and exit."),
  ("How did gana-sangha differ from monarchy?", "In ganas (Vrijji, Malla) the assembly took decisions by discussion or vote and chose the ruler; in monarchies the king was hereditary and the final authority."),
  ("Why was iron important?", "Iron tools expanded farming; iron weapons strengthened armies; both supported urban growth."),
  ("What were punch-marked coins?", "India's earliest silver coins, marked with punched symbols, made necessary by growing trade."),
  ("Explain the varna and jati systems.", "Varna: four Vedic classes (Brahmin, Kshatriya, Vaishya, Shudra); jati: occupation-based communities with inherited skills and own customs."),
  ("Name two major trade routes.", "Uttarapatha and Dakshinapatha."),
  ("Which three kingdoms rose in the south?", "The Cholas, Cheras and Pandyas.")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Harappan decline → ~1,000 years with little urban life → **Second Urbanisation** (1st millennium BCE, Ganga plains).",
  "**Janapadas → 16 mahajanapadas** (Magadha, Kosala, Vatsa, Avanti most powerful).",
  "**Sabha / samiti** advised the raja; **Vrijji and Malla** were early republics (ganas).",
  "**Iron, punch-marked coins**, trade routes and new ideas drove growth.",
  "**Varna** (four classes) and **jati** (occupation groups) organised society; early flexibility, later rigidity.",
  "**Uttarapatha / Dakshinapatha** linked regions; Cholas, Cheras, Pandyas rose in the south."])
